## Not enough to go round

Resources in a habitat are limited, so organisms **compete** for them. Competition happens **within** a species (two blackbirds fighting over a worm) and **between** species (blackbirds and thrushes both eating the worms).

**Plants compete for**

- **light** – for photosynthesis;
- **space** – to spread their leaves and roots;
- **water** – from the soil;
- **mineral ions** – such as nitrates from the soil.

**Animals compete for**

- **food**;
- **mates** – to reproduce;
- **territory** – an area with enough food, water and shelter to raise young.

The best competitors survive and reproduce, so competition drives natural selection.

**Interdependence**

Within a community, every species depends on others for **food**, **shelter**, **pollination** and **seed dispersal**. Bees depend on flowers for nectar; the flowers depend on bees for pollination; the birds that eat the bees depend on both. If one species is removed – say a disease wipes out the bees – the effects ripple through the whole community: fewer flowers set seed, so fewer plants grow next year, so there is less food for the caterpillars that feed on them, and so on. This is **interdependence**, and it is why ecologists worry about losing even a single species.

## Life at the limits

Some organisms live in conditions that would kill almost everything else. They are called **extremophiles**, and most of them are **bacteria** or other microorganisms. They are adapted to:

- **very high temperatures** – bacteria in hot springs and around deep-sea **hydrothermal vents** survive water above 80 °C thanks to enzymes that do not denature at those temperatures;
- **very high pressure** – organisms on the deep ocean floor cope with pressure hundreds of times greater than at the surface;
- **very high salt concentration** – microbes in salt lakes such as the Dead Sea are adapted so that they do not lose water by osmosis;
- extremes of **pH**, **cold** or **radiation**.

Extremophiles matter for two reasons. First, they show how far adaptation can go – life may be possible in environments on other planets that we once thought were lifeless. Second, their heat-stable enzymes are useful in industry and in the laboratory, for example in the process used to copy DNA.

The key exam point is simple: *extremophiles are organisms adapted to live in extreme conditions – name a condition and an example.*
